FAIRNESS 7 objection(s) · 2 serious
========================================================================

  [NEW · MINOR · CONTRADICTION] The draft calls the displayed composite score of 3.4 an error against a working of 3.35, but 3.35 rounds to 3.4 at one decimal place by standard rounding — there was no error.
     quote         : “Readers saw 3.4 ; the working underneath it comes to 3.35 , and that discrepancy is recorded below.”
     why           : 3.35 rounded to one decimal place is 3.4 under standard rounding rules. The draft characterises this as a discrepancy and implies a correction was made, but the displayed figure was arithmetically correct. Calling standard rounding an error is itself an error, and it implies the publication made a mistake it did not make.
     fix           : Either note that the displayed figure was a rounded version of 3.35 and was therefore correct, or explain what non-standard display convention was in use that made 3.4 wrong.

  [NEW · MINOR · CONTRADICTION] The change log repeats the same false framing of the rounding issue a second time.
     quote         : “The printed figure was out by 0.05 against its own working, for eight days, and nothing here had asked the question until the mark was applied. The number was corrected before the composite was abolished, and the correction is recorded here because it happened to a figure readers could see.”
     why           : Same error as above, stated again in the change log. 3.35 rounds to 3.4; the 'correction' described here was not a correction of an error but a change in display precision. Describing it as a correction implies the publication published a wrong number, which it did not.
     fix           : Replace 'The printed figure was out by 0.05 against its own working' with a note that the displayed figure was a rounded value and the change was to display more decimal places, not to correct an arithmetic error.

  [NEW · SERIOUS · CONTRADICTION] The section header 'Coverage has been merging them' is contradicted by the body's own finding that every outlet held attributed the figures correctly.
     quote         : “Coverage has been merging them. Different sizes, different patients, different rigour — and only one has published numbers.”
     why           : The body of the piece says 'Every outlet whose article we hold attributed those figures correctly to KEYNOTE-942.' The header 'Coverage has been merging them' is a claim about what coverage did that the piece itself retracts in the next section. A reader who sees only the header — or who reads the comparison table — comes away with a false impression of what the outlets did. The piece has already corrected this characterisation in its text; the header has not been updated to match.
     fix           : Change the header to something like 'The two trials, side by side' (which the draft already uses as a sub-label) and remove the claim 'Coverage has been merging them' from the header, since the body retracts it.

  [NEW · MINOR · THIRD_PARTY] The draft characterises Morning Glory Sciences negatively as an outlet 'we can find no other publication citing,' which is a claim about the outlet's standing offered without evidence beyond the draft's own search.
     quote         : “Morning Glory Sciences — an outlet we can find no other publication citing, so we give the argument on its merits rather than on its authority”
     why           : This is a negative characterisation of a named outlet's credibility. The draft's search may simply have been incomplete, or the outlet may be new or niche. The characterisation could be unfair and is not supported by any evidence beyond the absence of results in the draft's own search. The draft is transparent about its reasoning, but the negative framing still stands in the published text.
     fix           : Replace with a neutral description: 'Morning Glory Sciences — a specialist outlet whose reach we could not independently assess — ' or simply omit the credibility characterisation and let the argument stand on its merits as the draft already says it intends to.

  [NEW · SERIOUS · THIRD_PARTY] The draft claims 'Neither reading reached the general coverage' without holding or citing any general coverage to support that claim.
     quote         : “Neither reading reached the general coverage.”
     why           : This is a claim about what general coverage did or did not report. The draft's own source list contains only specialist outlets. The draft has no basis for asserting what general coverage did or did not include, and the claim is not qualified as being limited to what the draft holds. This is the same class of error the draft itself corrected in an earlier version regarding specialist outlets.
     fix           : Add a scope qualifier: 'Neither reading appeared in the general coverage we hold' or, if no general coverage was held, remove the sentence or replace it with 'We hold no general coverage of this announcement.'

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] The short version describes INTerpath-001 as 'the blinded study of 1,137' when the trial is double-blind, a materially stronger design than 'blinded' alone implies.
     quote         : “an open-label study of 157 patients, not the blinded study of 1,137 that was announced”
     why           : The contrast being drawn is between open-label and double-blind. Describing INTerpath-001 only as 'blinded' understates its design and weakens the contrast the sentence is making. The body of the piece correctly calls it 'double-blind' throughout. The short version should match.
     fix           : Change 'the blinded study of 1,137' to 'the double-blind study of 1,137.'

  [NEW · MINOR · THIRD_PARTY] The draft says 'Several outlets called it a landmark' without naming which outlets or providing any evidence for the characterisation.
     quote         : “Several outlets called it a landmark.”
     why           : This is a claim about what named or unnamed outlets said. The draft names every outlet it holds in the section that follows, and none of them is cited as having used the word 'landmark.' If the draft holds evidence for this claim, it should cite it; if it does not, the claim is unsupported.
     fix           : Either name the outlets that used 'landmark' and cite the articles, or remove the sentence.

