INFERENCE 5 finding(s) · 1 serious
========================================================================

  [NEW · SERIOUS · CONTRADICTION] The draft itself says in the same section that 'the interval stopped including 1.0 rather than crossing a threshold anyone tested' — using 'crossing' here to mean a threshold was crossed contradicts that explicit denial, and a reader quoting this sentence alone would take it to mean a prespecified statistical threshold was crossed at year three.
     quote         : “the crossing was resolved by year three, not year five”
     evidence says : The 95% CI stopped including 1.0 by year three (a descriptive observation), but no prespecified threshold was crossed at any follow-up point in the descriptive analyses; the primary threshold was crossed at the original 2023 primary analysis.
     fix           : So the interval cleared the no-effect line by year three, not year five, and what the last two years added was two more years of it holding rather than a sharper measurement.

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] The draft has just explained that the two-sided p = 0.053 and the one-sided p = 0.0266 are 'identical evidence, measured against a different question'; calling the CI 'the tell' implies it reveals which framing is operative, but the 95% CI crossing 1.0 is simply the mathematical equivalent of two-sided p > 0.05 — it does not independently reveal anything the p-values do not already show, and the draft's own explanation has already made the distinction clearly.
     quote         : “The confidence interval is the tell: 0.309–1.017 crosses 1.0, which is exactly what a two-sided 95% interval keys on — the convention journals print, and not the threshold this trial set for itself”
     evidence says : The 95% CI crossing 1.0 is equivalent to two-sided p > 0.05, which is consistent with the journal's two-sided framing; it is not an independent signal but a restatement of the same result on a different scale.
     fix           : The confidence interval makes the same point on a different scale: 0.309–1.017 crosses 1.0, which is the two-sided 95% convention journals print — and not the threshold this trial set for itself.

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] The draft has just stated that the five-year analyses 'were descriptive: they were not designed to test a hypothesis'; 'more certainty' implies accumulating inferential weight, but in a descriptive analysis additional follow-up adds consistency of observation, not formal inferential certainty, and the sentence does not make that distinction.
     quote         : “More data, more certainty, and a recurrence effect that held up rather than fading — one of the better signs that an effect is real.”
     evidence says : The interval staying clear of 1.0 over two additional years of descriptive follow-up is consistent with a real effect, but this is an observation about consistency, not an increase in inferential certainty from a formal test.
     fix           : More data, a consistent picture, and a recurrence effect that held up rather than fading — one of the better signs that an effect is real, even in a descriptive analysis.

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] 'Wide margin' is an unanchored qualitative characterisation: 0.0266 is roughly one-quarter of the prespecified threshold of 0.10, which is a meaningful distance but the draft provides no basis for calling it wide, and a reader could take 'wide margin' to imply a stronger result than the numbers carry.
     quote         : “so 0.0266 was inside its own prespecified threshold by a wide margin”
     evidence says : 0.0266 is well inside the prespecified one-sided alpha of 0.10; whether the margin is 'wide' depends on a standard the draft does not supply.
     fix           : so 0.0266 was inside its own prespecified threshold of 0.10 — roughly one-quarter of it — and 0.053 is a near miss only against a line this trial never used.

  [NEW · MINOR · CONTRADICTION] The corrections section states that the five-year rates 'now rest on' the ASCO abstract (obtained by hand on 3 September), not on The ASCO Post report; the body text has not been updated to reflect this change in attribution and still implies the source is a secondary report rather than the primary abstract.
     quote         : “which is why the five-year rates above come from the report of the analysis rather than from the paper”
     evidence says : Per the corrections section, the five-year rates now rest on the ASCO abstract (abstract 9500), which is a primary source; the body text should name it.
     fix           : which is why the five-year rates above come from the ASCO abstract (abstract 9500) rather than from the paper.

