  Pharmacy Times
     got right     : Stated that 'the companies have not yet disclosed the magnitude of benefit, hazard ratios, event rates, or detailed safety findings.' Noted that 'High-risk resected stage IIB/IIC melanoma carries recurrence and melanoma-specific mortality comparable to stage III' — the counter-argument to the lower-absolute-benefit concern. Listed remaining readouts including 'absolute benefit, stage/biomarker subgroup effects, discontinuation and feasibility metrics, and overall survival.' Attributed the 49%/59% figures to the KEYNOTE-942 five-year update.
     still omitted : Does not discuss the OS confidence interval width or the n=14 death count, does not address the open-label design of the Phase 2b as a limitation, and does not explain the one-sided/two-sided p-value distinction.
     https://www.pharmacytimes.com/view/phase-3-trial-marks-first-success-for-personalized-mrna-cancer-therapy-in-resected-melanoma

  EMJ Reviews (European Medical Journal)
     got right     : Stated that 'numerical efficacy estimates, confidence intervals, follow-up duration, and detailed subgroup findings were not disclosed.' Noted that 'detailed efficacy and safety data, overall survival results, presentation at a medical meeting, and regulatory review will be necessary before its potential clinical role can be fully established.' Correctly described the trial as enrolling 1,137 patients.
     still omitted : Does not attribute the 49%/59% figures to the Phase 2b at all — they are not mentioned — so the substitution problem the draft identifies is avoided by omission rather than by explicit attribution. No discussion of OS interval width, n=14 death count, open-label design difference, or p-value framing.
